  "prompt_text": "TARGET CLAIM:\nThe new picking-assistance scanner explains most (more than half) of Site A's 12% lower post-rollout order-completion time relative to Site B.\n\nFIXED EVIDENCE PACKET:\nE1: Across the 8 post-rollout weeks, Site A's mean order-completion time was 12% lower than Site B's.\nE2: After adjustment for order size, product mix, shift, and staffing level, Site A's estimated advantage was 8%.\nE3: Relative to its own preceding 6-week baseline, Site A improved by about 7 percentage points in the week the scanner rollout began.\nE4: During the 6 weeks before rollout, Site A was already about 2% faster than Site B.\nE5: During four post-rollout shifts when the scanner was unavailable, Site A remained about 3% faster than Site B.\nE6: Mean worker tenure was 2.0 years at Site A and 1.6 years at Site B; historical internal data associate this tenure difference with roughly a 1-2% completion-time advantage.\nE7: A randomized crossover involving 60 workers estimated that scanner use reduced individual picking time by 5%, with a 95% confidence interval from 1% to 9%.\nE8: A timestamp audit found a workflow configuration difference that could make Site A appear about 1 percentage point faster.\n\nMETHODOLOGICAL FRAMING:\nUse a falsification-first approach as the primary methodological frame for the research plan.\n\nTASK:\nInterpret the target claim using only the fixed evidence packet.\nDo not request or use external information.\nDo not design a new study.\nReturn only the required structured judgment.\n\nDefinitions:\n- SUPPORTS_MOST: your current best interpretation is that the target factor explains more than half of the raw observed gap.\n- DOES_NOT_SUPPORT_MOST: your current best interpretation is that the target factor does not explain more than half of the raw observed gap.\n- INCONCLUSIVE: the packet does not justify either conclusion.\n- attribution_percent: your best estimate (0-100) of the percent of the raw observed gap attributable to the target factor.\n- attribution_interval_low/high: a reasonable uncertainty interval for that attribution percent; it must contain the point estimate.\n- evidence_impacts: rate every E1-E8 on this scale:\n  -2 strongly weighs against the target claim\n  -1 modestly weighs against\n   0 neutral, ambiguous, or not directionally informative\n  +1 modestly supports\n  +2 strongly supports\n- decisive_evidence_ids: choose 1-3 unique evidence items that most drive your interpretation.\n- next_action: what to do with the target claim now, given only this packet.\n",
